criteria.descriptionMarkdown:
A public test-support API provides a deterministic real text system (Cosmic Text without system fonts, loaded with `gpux_fonts::ALL`, primary family IBM Plex Sans) on Windows, Linux and macOS. `#[gpux::test(text = "bundled")]` and an explicit `TestAppContext` constructor use it.

A new test group asserts exact values for fixed inputs on every desktop OS:
- glyph advances and line heights for ASCII strings in IBM Plex Sans and Lilex;
- wrap points at a fixed width;
- ellipsis truncation results;
- multi-run styled text widths.

No system font is consulted (a missing family falls back to bundled fonts, not to system fonts).

These read time only from `BackgroundExecutor::now()` (the scheduler clock):
- touch gesture recognition (tap, double tap, long press, pan, fling momentum) and wheel filtering;
- `ScrollActivity` (programmatic smooth scroll, fling, retarget);
- scrollbar timers and repeat, where gpui has them (it has none: it only publishes thumb offsets in the input turn, and scrollbar chrome with its timers belongs to consumers);
- `with_animation` and `animated_frame`;
- list and div scroll momentum;
- editable-text programmatic scroll;
- image loading timers.

A new test group proves, under `TestAppContext` and with `advance_clock` only (no sleeping):
- a long press fires at its threshold and not before;
- a fling decays to rest at a deterministic offset;
- a smooth programmatic scroll reaches its target at its duration;
- an animation reports exact progress at fixed times;
- reduced motion still bypasses motion.
Two runs with the same seed produce identical offsets.

Test-support APIs simulate the following, all delivered through the same `Window::dispatch_event`/input-handler path real platforms use:
- IME composition: set marked text, update it, commit, cancel;
- key-up events;
- multi-click with an explicit click count (double and triple);
- drags along a path with a held button and modifiers.

A new test group asserts the resulting events, selection and composition state on an editable text element and on a drag target.

The port follows the gpux skill's single-PR import procedure (`references/update-from-zed-pr.md`):
- commit bodies carry the Zed source PRs and commits;
- the import ledgers record #64718 and #64842 as ported with gpux adaptations;
- the adaptation (gpux renderer differences, Windows support that upstream lacks) is described for future upstream imports.

Verified by review.

`gpux-conformance` provides image comparison with these features:
- relative probes: a color at a point within a tolerance, region uniformity, two regions equal or different, a before/after change inside a region;
- golden comparison with a per-channel tolerance (default ±3) and a maximum differing-pixel ratio;
- per-backend golden names `<scene>@<scale>x.<backend>.png`, with backend ids such as `lavapipe`, `warp`, `metal`, `swiftshader-webgpu`;
- regeneration with `GPUX_UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1`;
- on failure, actual, expected and diff images written under `artifacts-temp/`.

A new test group covers pass, fail and update paths with synthetic images.

The `gpux-conformance` crate holds a catalog of at least 30 deterministic scenes. Each declares id, covered areas, logical size, scale factors, lanes, interaction script, semantic expectations and pixel probes.

Together the scenes cover:
- **layout:** flex, grid, absolute, overflow, fixed, sticky (including stacking and push-out);
- **text:** shaping, runs, decorations, wrapping, truncation, selection with interactive text;
- **editing:** editable text and IME;
- **lists:** `uniform_list` and `list` virtualization;
- **scrolling:** wheel, nested wheel propagation, scrollbars, programmatic smooth scroll, touch pan and fling;
- **focus and actions:** focus and tab order, actions and layered keymaps, hover and active styles;
- **interaction:** drag and drop, tooltips, anchored and unclipped deferred overlays;
- **media and paint:** raster images, SVG, canvas paths, borders (solid, dashed, radii), shadows, gradients, opacity and clipping;
- **effects:** frame effects (effect, animated and transition frames) and shader effects where supported;
- **color and semantics:** sRGB compositing swatches, the accessibility tree and devtools semantics.

A coverage test fails when a required area has no scene or when scene ids collide.

CI runs lane C on:
- `ubuntu-24.04` with Mesa lavapipe (`mesa-vulkan-drivers`, selected with `VK_DRIVER_FILES`);
- `windows-2025` with WARP;
- `macos-15` with Metal, once the sibling macOS epic lands.

Goldens are committed for `lavapipe`, `warp` and `metal`, and failure artifacts are uploaded. Verified by a green workflow run.

`gpux-testkit lane d` launches the conformance desktop host on the real platform (`gpui_platform::application()`) for the lane-D scenes.

The run checks that windows open and present, then asserts:
- probes from `render_to_image` on the real surface;
- resize and scale-factor changes;
- a clipboard round trip;
- closing without leaks or panics;
- OS-level synthetic input where the OS permits it (XTest through `xdotool` on X11 under Xvfb, `SendInput` on Windows), translated into the expected GPUI events. Other scripts inject input in-app.

The summary line counts the results. The run passes locally on Windows.

gpux has its own diagnostics surface:
- a `diagnostics` feature on `gpux`, `gpux-wgpu-renderer` and `gpux-web`;
- `__gpux*` JS globals;
- the `?gpux-html-island=1` opt-in.

The old names stay as working aliases: `rayx_diagnostics` is an alias feature, `__rayx*` globals are still published under the alias, and `?rayx-html-island=1` is still honoured. Diagnostics stay content-free and out of production builds. A source-guard test group asserts the alias mapping.

A Playwright harness in `tooling/web-e2e` (committed lockfile), started through `gpux-testkit web e2e`, defines launch profiles:
- `ci-linux-swiftshader`: headless, with `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan,CDPScreenshotNewSurface,... --use-angle=swiftshader --use-vulkan=swiftshader`;
- `local-gpu`: new headless (`channel: 'chromium'`) or `--headed`;
- `windows-software`: optional, `--use-angle=d3d11-warp` or a WARP LUID.

Fixtures that every spec uses:
- fail when the app reports a WebGL backend while WebGPU is required, and assert the forced-WebGL2 backend where requested;
- assert `crossOriginIsolated`;
- fail on console errors and panics.

`@canary` specs prove startup on WebGPU, a non-flat canvas screenshot and a located click.

`@pr`/`@nightly`/`@dpr2`-tagged specs cover each `gpui_web` area against conformance scenes, through trusted CDP input and screenshot probes:
- backend selection with forced WebGL2 and stubbed `navigator.gpu`;
- canvas sizing, DPR 1/2 and ResizeObserver;
- the frame loop and window-close cleanup;
- pointer and mouse events (buttons, capture, enter/leave), and wheel events across all three delta modes;
- keyboard events (modifiers, repeat, AltGr), and touch (tap, pan, fling, cancel);
- IME composition through `Input.imeSetComposition`;
- clipboard copy/paste through trusted events and the fallback path;
- `html_element` overlays and HTML islands (opt-in);
- canvas text fallback, taking over `test_canvas_fallback.mjs`;
- the dispatcher and worker queue, taking over `test_queue.mjs`;
- SVG and asset loading, cursor styles, focus/blur/visibility, and viewport safe areas;
- a Dawn-validation smoke that renders every pipeline with no validation errors.

Timing-threshold specs carry `@timing` and run only in lane G.

`gpux-testkit android run` installs and launches the host on the first available device or emulator (or `--serial`), runs the lane-E scenes and collects the results. It asserts:
- startup and the backend actually selected (Vulkan, or the GL fallback, recorded);
- surface loss and recovery across pause/resume;
- touch translation through `adb shell input tap/swipe`, including a pan and a fling;
- `render_to_image` probes.

Logcat result markers are parsed into the summary line, and PNGs are pulled into `artifacts-temp/`.

`gpux-testkit ios run --simulator` boots a pinned Simulator, installs and launches the host, and runs the lane-F scenes. It asserts:
- startup;
- Metal through wgpu;
- CoreText text metrics within tolerance;
- lifecycle (background/foreground);
- `render_to_image` probes;
- in-app touch injection.

It parses console markers (`simctl launch --console-pty`) into the summary line, and copies PNGs from the app container. If Metal is unavailable in the hosted Simulator, the run reports pixel checks as skipped with the reason, and local-device runs keep them. Verified on a Mac or in the macOS CI job.

On a Mac, `gpux-testkit lane a c d` passes:
- lane A on the test platform;
- lane C through `MetalHeadlessRenderer` with committed `metal` goldens;
- lane D in real AppKit windows with in-app input injection.

Verified on a Mac or in the macOS CI jobs.

`gpux-testkit lane g` runs on the developer's hardware GPU (Windows DX12 primary; Linux and macOS when run there):
- lane B in the `local-gpu` profile (headed with `--headed`), including `@timing` and `@dpr2`;
- lanes C and D on the hardware adapter, with relative probes only;
- the gpux benchmarks (`gpux-platform`'s `randomized_element_tree` Criterion suite and `#[gpux::bench]` benches).

It writes a timing summary under `artifacts-temp/testkit/g/`. `lane g --list` shows the selection. Verified by a local run.

The following describe the gpux lanes and `gpux-testkit` instead of RayX proof, and keep RayX only as a consumer:
- `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` (Validation, Consumers, AgentX note);
- `.claude/skills/gpux/SKILL.md` (validation routing);
- `.claude/skills/gpux/references/multithreaded-wasm-apps.md`, which builds threaded WASM with `gpux-testkit web build`;
- `docs/installation-and-usage.md`, with its RayX/.NET-specific notes removed or marked consumer-only.

`LINUX-WSL.md` gains the gpux lane commands for WSL, and its RayX sections are marked consumer-only. Verified by review.
